h9_quantile_function_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is another name for the quantile function?","options":["Probability mass function","Standard deviation","Inverse CDF","Sample mean"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"78622aaa8678e52895f1a679"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a quantile function tell you?","options":["The probability of every exact point only.","The largest sample-space event","The value at a chosen cumulative probability level.","The variance around the mean only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac758829f93746ee50519950"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of inverse is the quantile function?","options":["A reciprocal of the variance","A complement of an event","A derivative of a density","A generalized inverse of the CDF"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5763edeb0c03da29a8d05a0f"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the median in quantile language?","options":["The quantile at probability zero","The quantile at probability one half","The maximum possible value","The variance at the mean"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"453fc39a3422a0be3f0816e0"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does the ninety-fifth percentile of delivery time represent?","options":["The variance of delivery time","The probability of zero delivery time","A quantile of the delivery-time distribution","The sample size of delivery observations"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eab93e4e596a9994337ebe3d"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $F(x)=x$ on $[0,1]$, what is $Q(0.25)$?","options":["$0.75$","$1$","$0$","$0.25$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7551611929f24d3cb97ea72f"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What role does a quantile function play in simulation?","options":["It removes the need for any random numbers","It turns every distribution into a degenerate value.","It computes only sample variance","It turns uniform numbers into samples of a target distribution."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"39ca31ad165deb4d62f4f448"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which input is appropriate for $Q(u)$?","options":["$u=-2$","$u=1.5$","$u=\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ with no probability scale","$u=0.8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cda563cc336574ff9c5e4e25"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition use an infimum?","options":["It forces all distributions to be uniform","It computes a variance directly","It handles CDFs that are flat or have jumps","It makes the quantile always negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9240d5ac2ef2f20a3f6d374f"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How are quantiles related to percentiles?","options":["Percentiles are unrelated to CDFs","Quantiles are only variances","Percentiles are quantiles at percentage levels.","Quantiles apply only to impossible events."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"59ad623a00331b0a2128316f"},{"id":"pt_batch9_quantile_function_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the quantile function useful for describing distribution positions?","options":["It identifies values by cumulative level.","It lists outcomes alphabetically","It gives only the expected value","It removes the CDF from the model."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revisi